In the 199th year after the town was founded, under the third full moon, Will Parris and Sansa Parris had a child, Victor Parris;
In the 203rd year after the town was founded, on the sixth day after the autumn equinox, Will Parris and Sansa Parris had a child, Lennard Parris;
In the 206th year after the town was founded, at the Start of Winter, Will Parris and Sansa Parris had a child, Marianne Parris;
Two days after the winter solstice, Marianne Parris died in infancy;
In the 207th year after the town was founded, at the winter solstice, Will Parris sued Cannon Putnam over a land dispute. After Viscount Leon's judgment, Will Parris won the case;
In the 208th year after the town was founded, during the sowing season, Sansa Parris gave birth to Raven Parris;
In the 211th year after the town was founded, during the wheat harvest, Victor Parris earned the favor of Confessor George and Viscount Leon, and went to the city to study;
In the 213th year after the town was founded, before the autumn harvest, Victor Parris graduated from the seminary and became Viscount Leon's manservant;
In the 218th year after the town was founded, at Midsummer, Sansa Parris gave birth to Lyanna Parris;
In the 220th year after the town was founded, when the hawthorn bloomed, Lennard Parris served in the army as a spearman;
In the 221st year after the town was founded, when the acorns ripened, Lennard Parris was killed in battle;
In the 225th year after the town was founded, at Michaelmas, Will Parris drowned;
In the 227th year after the town was founded, after the first plowing, Victor Parris became the manor steward of Viscount Leon;
When the violets bloomed, Raven Parris married David Kovik, Viscount Leon's manservant, and took the name Raven Kovik;
In the 229th year after the town was founded, when the hawthorn bloomed, Lyanna Parris died of the plague;
In the 231st year after the town was founded, Victor Parris married Alice Sterling, Viscount Leon's maid. Alice Sterling took the name Alice Parris;
In the 237th year after the town was founded, after the first full moon following the summer solstice, Victor Parris and Alice Parris had a child, Jean Parris. The midwives were Vivian Joan and Rebecca Joan;
In the 238th year after the town was founded, following the winter solstice, Jean Parris died of pneumonia;
In the 244th year after the town was founded, Victor Parris left the manor and returned to St. Arbor. Viscount Leon rewarded him with the oak grove on the southern slope of Mount St. Arbor and all the land stretching from the oak grove to the river;
In the 246th year after the town was founded, Alice Parris died from a snakebite;
In the 248th year after the town was founded, after the first spring rain, the widow Martha Garcia married Victor Parris and took the name Martha Parris. Her land and property were incorporated into the Parris family holdings;
In the 250th year after the town was founded, after the first spring rain, Victor Parris and Martha Parris had a child, Jackson Parris. The midwife was Rebecca Fran;
In the 252nd year after the town was founded, before winter began, Victor Parris and Martha Parris had a child, Betty Parris. The midwife was Rebecca Fran;
In the 260th year after the town was founded, when the sunflowers bloomed, Jackson Parris died of the plague;
In the 266th year after the town was founded, Confessor George and Magistrate Victor returned to Annie the inheritance left to her by the Fran couple. In gratitude, Annie promised to provide the Magistrate's family with free herbal remedies for the next three years;

In the 201st year after the town was founded, when the river thawed, five-year-old Giles Warren found Sansa Parris fallen by the roadside and ran to notify Will Parris, earning commendations from Magistrate Garcia and Confessor George;
In the 211th year after the town was founded, at wheat harvest, Giles Warren was recommended by Confessor George and Magistrate Garcia to accompany Victor Parris to the city for his studies;
In the 213th year after the town was founded, before the autumn harvest, Giles Warren returned to town to work at the lord's manor;
In the 220th year after the town was founded, after the autumn harvest, Giles Warren left the manor and purchased twenty fields;
In the 227th year after the town was founded, when the wheat seedlings once again turned green, Giles Warren married Beatrice Warren and purchased a property with a courtyard in town;
In the 230th year after the town was founded, after the first snow, Old Green Warren and his wife Ivy Warren were mauled to death by a starving bear that had come down from the mountains;
As their only son, Giles Warren inherited all of his parents' estate;
In the 260th year after the town was founded, when the sunflowers bloomed, Beatrice Warren died of plague;
In the 261st year after the town was founded, Giles Warren was imprisoned for gambling debts and released after selling his fields to repay them;
In the 265th year after the town was founded, Giles Warren was imprisoned for debts incurred from drinking and released after selling his livestock to repay them;

In the 200th year after the town was founded, in late autumn, Cannon Putnam and Daisy Putnam had a son, Rand Putnam;
In the 206th year after the town was founded, after the snow began to fall, Cannon Putnam lent Will Parris twenty silver coins, secured against Will Parris's twenty fields;
In the 211th year after the town was founded, after New Year's, Cannon Putnam sent his only son, Rand Putnam, to learn carpentry;
In the 221st year after the town was founded, before Midsummer, Rand Putnam returned to town, purchased two tracts of woodland, and established a carpenter's workshop;
In the 224th year after the town was founded, at New Year's, Rand Putnam and Raven Parris entered marriage negotiations, but the arrangement fell through due to a land dispute between the two families;
In the 225th year after the town was founded, when the snow melted, Cannon Putnam and Will Parris sued each other over a land dispute. After Confessor George mediated, they finally reconciled;
After Michaelmas, Cannon Putnam was imprisoned on suspicion of murdering Will Parris;
In the 226th year after the town was founded, after New Year's, Cannon Putnam was acquitted and released;
In the 227th year after the town was founded, when the violets bloomed, Rand Putnam married Odette Martin, maid to Viscount Leon. Odette Martin took the name Odette Putnam;
In the 229th year after the town was founded, when the hawthorn bloomed, Daisy Putnam died of plague;
In the 231st year after the town was founded, in early summer, Rand Putnam and Odette Putnam had a child, Faithful Putnam;
In the 236th year after the town was founded, after New Year's, Rand Putnam and Odette Putnam had a son, Billy Putnam, with Midwives Vivian Joan and Rebecca Joan in attendance;
In the 256th year after the town was founded, in midsummer, Rand Putnam drowned in a flood;
After the first frost, the Putnam family inventoried their fields and holdings. The brothers Billy and Faithful came to blows over the division of the inheritance, but reconciled after Confessor George and their mother, Odette, urged them to make peace;
In the 259th year after the town was founded, Billy Putnam sought to lease the mill, but Viscount Leon refused;
In the 260th year after the town was founded, when the sunflowers bloomed, Odette Putnam died of plague
In the 264th year after the town was founded, in deep winter, Billy's maid was killed when a log fell on her while she was moving firewood. Billy paid her family five silver coins;

In the 256th year after the town was founded, in late summer, Pickett Corey and Lizzie Corey moved to St. Arbor with a loom, wooden crates, and two head of livestock, renting an old house by the river;
In the 257th year, when the chamomile bloomed, Pickett Corey and Lizzie Corey had twin sons, Edmund Corey and Elijah Corey, delivered by Midwife Rebecca Fran;
In the 258th year, after autumn began, Pickett earned the steward's favor by repairing the old waterwheel and was permitted to manage the mill temporarily;
In the 259th year, in spring, at Magistrate Victor's recommendation, Viscount Leon entrusted the mill to Pickett Corey's management, requiring him to remit forty percent of the mill's income each year.
In the 260th year, when the sunflowers bloomed, Edmund Corey and Elijah Corey died of the plague;
In the 261st year, in winter, the haystack in the Corey family's yard caught fire. Lizzie suspected arson, but the matter was dropped for lack of witnesses;
In the 262nd year, at the beginning of spring, Pickett fenced off the vacant land by the riverside to plant wheat, and Billy entered into a dispute with him, citing the unclear old boundary between their land;

In the 214th year after the town was founded, Faler Fran and Lacey Fran had a son, Will Fran;
In the 221st year after the town was founded, when the dandelions were in full bloom, Lacey Fran died of illness;
In the 236th year after the town was founded, Midwife Vivian Joan and her daughter Rebecca Joan came to town at the invitation of Confessor George, and temporarily took up residence in Doctor Fran's clinic;
In the 237th year after the town was founded, Alice Parris had a difficult labor. Vivian and Rebecca assisted her, and both mother and child survived. Magistrate Garcia and Confessor George rewarded them;
In the 240th year after the town was founded, after the first heavy snowfall, Vivian Joan passed away;
In the 241st year after the town was founded, when the violets were in bloom, Will Fran married Rebecca Joan, who took the name Rebecca Fran;
In the 243rd year after the town was founded, after the New Year, Faler Fran passed away, and his only son, Will Fran, inherited the Fran family clinic;
In the 245th year after the town was founded, in midsummer, Rebecca Fran gave birth to Annie Fran;
In the 260th year after the town was founded, when the sunflowers were in bloom, Rebecca Fran and Will Fran died of the plague;
According to the will, until Annie came of age, the Fran family's estate was jointly managed by Confessor George and Magistrate Victor;
In the 266th year after the town was founded, Confessor George and Magistrate Victor returned the estate left by the Frans to Annie Fran;
Annie Fran took in two strangers;

In the 227th year after the town was founded, 15-year-old Lake Ernst, cousin of bakery owner Inman Wall, came to work as an apprentice at the Walls' bakery;
In the 231st year after the town was founded, when the violets bloomed, Lake Ernst married Cassandra Nikolaus from a neighboring town, and Cassandra Nikolaus changed her name to Cassandra Ernst;
In the 232nd year after the town was founded, on the day of the first spring storm, Lake Ernst adopted an abandoned baby left at the door and named him Carter Ernst;
In the 233rd year after the town was founded, after New Year's, Inman Wall and Caitlin Wall had a son, Louis Wall;
In the 234th year after the town was founded, in the depths of winter, Lake Ernst died in the cellar in the dark;
Before New Year's in the 235th year after the town was founded, Cassandra Ernst died in childbirth after giving birth to her and Lake Ernst's daughter, Dot Ernst;
The Walls adopted Carter Ernst and Dot Ernst;
After New Year's in the 248th year after the town was founded, Carter Ernst was imprisoned for gambling debts. After Dot Ernst borrowed money from the Walls to pay the debt, Carter Ernst was released;
Before the first snow, Carter Ernst fled the town because of his gambling debts and joined a merchant caravan bound overseas;
After the first snow, Inman Wall died of illness;
Before New Year's in the 249th year after the town was founded, Carter Ernst returned to town with profits from trading and hired Rand Putnam to build a tavern;
Dot Ernst began traveling to a neighboring town to learn brewing;
In the height of summer in the 250th year after the town was founded, while Viscount Leon and his family were away on a trip, Carter Ernst was caught stealing the viscount's jewelry by a male servant. The servant was killed, and Carter Ernst was sentenced to be hanged;
In the 252nd year after the town was founded, Louis Wall married Dot Ernst, and Dot Ernst changed her name to Dot Wall;
In the height of summer in the 254th year after the town was founded, Louis Wall and Dot Wall had a son, White Wall, with Midwife Rebecca Fran assisting at the birth;
White Wall died of illness on the coldest snowy day;
Before New Year's in the 255th year after the town was founded, Caitlin Wall died of illness;
In early autumn in the 256th year after the town was founded, Louis Wall and Dot Wall had a daughter, Alice Wall, with the midwife Rebecca Fran assisting at the birth;
Alice Wall died of illness during the autumn harvest;
When the hawthorn bloomed in the 258th year after the town was founded, Louis Wall and Dot Wall had a daughter, Mary Wall, with Midwife Rebecca Fran assisting at the birth;
After the autumn harvest in the 260th year after the town was founded, Louis Wall sued Pickett Corey, suspecting him of hiding flour in the mill. After Confessor George mediated, the two made peace;
In the height of summer in the 262nd year after the town was founded, the Walls' tavern was sealed by the constable on suspicion of fencing stolen goods;
After the autumn harvest, the investigation ended and the Walls' tavern reopened.

In the 211th year after the town was founded, after New Year, Viscount Lockhart mortgaged the land west of Lake Hill as far as the thorn thickets to Viscount Leon; the serfs bound to this land, Clyde Williams and his wife Bonnie Williams, moved to St. Arbor.
In the 213th year after the town was founded, Clyde and Bonnie tended the lord's sheep west of town, agreeing to pay their rent with a portion of the wool and cheese;
In the 219th year after the town was founded, when the daisies were in bloom, Bonnie Williams gave birth to John Williams;
In the 226th year after the town was founded, at the beginning of summer, John began herding sheep with his father, learning the hillsides, streams, and paths leading into the forest;
In the 227th year after the town was founded, on the third day after New Year, Bonnie Williams gave birth to Joseph Williams;
In the 238th year after the town was founded, John Williams married Vida Shelby, a shepherdess from a neighboring village. Vida Shelby took the name Vida Williams;
In the 240th year after the town was founded, when the floods came, Clyde Williams fell into the river and drowned while protecting the lord's flock;
Viscount Leon, the lord, rewarded the Williams family with land, freeing them from serfdom;
In the 242nd year after the town was founded, John Williams leased lifetime use of the pasture on the southern hillside;
In the 246th year after the town was founded, Joseph Williams broke his right leg in a blizzard while trying to find a lost sheep;
In the 256th year after the town was founded, in early spring, after years of poor harvests, John Williams mortgaged the pasture to Billy Putnam, repaying the interest by grazing livestock there;
In the 260th year after the town was founded, when the hawthorns were in bloom, John Williams's cousin Scott Williams died of the plague, and Scott Williams's daughter Abigail came to town to seek refuge with the Williams family;

In the 236th year after the town was founded, early summer, Old Levis Terry and Nancy Terry had Levis Terry;
In the 241st year after the town was founded, midsummer, Old Levis Terry and Nancy Terry had Parker Terry;
In the 245th year after the town was founded, late spring, Old Levis Terry and Nancy Terry had Pamela Terry;
In the 247th year after the town was founded, when the snow melted, Pamela Terry died of smallpox;
In the 254th year after the town was founded, at the Midsummer Festival, Levis began repairing plowshares, horseshoes, and harvesting tools for the villagers;
In the 257th year after the town was founded, after the autumn harvest, the younger Terry brother also apprenticed at the smithy, and the two brothers tended the forge together;
In the 260th year after the town was founded, when the sunflowers bloomed, Old Levis Terry, Nancy Terry, and Parker Terry died of the plague;
The Terry family smithy was inherited by Levis Terry;

Ninth Full-Moon Night
Overseas spices, four jars, supplied by First Mate Jack, sold for eighty silver coins;
Giles owes twelve chips;
Giles owes three cups of rum;
Two bolts of purple silk, snagged on the outside, supplied by Scarface Hanks, sold for thirty-two silver coins;
One gold tooth brought back from the city by Faithful, exchanged for ten chips;
Scarface Hanks provided raiding routes used by soldiers from a neighboring country, sold for three silver coins;
Tonight's alcohol revenue: six silver coins and eight copper coins; chip revenue: eight silver coins and one gold tooth;
Next Full-Moon Night: pay First Mate Jack seventy-five silver coins, Scarface Hanks twenty-four silver coins; nearby patrol constable, two silver coins;
